  "slug": "social-mice-6af3",
  "summary": "Newly posted preprints and a published study report divergent light-driven and sleep-related behaviors among closely related strains and species.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is behavioral divergence among genetically similar lines or species, with light input and sleep-related states as recurring variables. In Hydra vulgaris, two closely related F1 strains from the AEP line differ in phototaxis under starvation: AEP Irvine wanders, whereas AEP Kiel shows positive phototaxis. In Drosophila, cold-induced dormancy promotes large huddles away from food, and NPF signaling regulates both feeding and huddling, with the two separable from appetite-driven effects. Artificially selected early and late eclosion chronotype lines show correlated divergence in sleep architecture, sleep fragmentation and recovery. A published study reports species-specific photopreference of Drosophila species for rhythmic flickering light beyond the perceptual flicker frequency. The new element is the dissociation of behavioral outputs, including aggregation site choice from feeding and sleep architecture linked to chronotype.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
